{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# fsck should RECOVER, not just refuse\n\nChange 1557 (bug b636) makes the snapshot rebuild FAIL CLOSED when the prior SNAPSHOT cites\nsource events absent from disk. That stops new corruption, but it leaves the existing\npopulation permanently un-repairable: a dry run of the fixed fsck reports 505 live tickets\nin exactly that shape, and `fsck --repair` can now never drive them to fsck-zero.\n\n## Evidence that recovery is possible\nThe deleted event files are still in `origin/tickets` history (compaction deleted them from\nthe worktree; the blobs remain reachable at the deleting commit's parent). Recovery therefore\nneeds no new data structure — a per-ticket `git log --diff-filter=D -- <tid>/` scan enumerates\nevery event file ever deleted from that ticket, and each blob is read at the deleting commit's\nparent. This is the same `git show <ref>:<path>` blob-recovery idiom the repo already uses in\n`binding_store.py` and `_store/compat.py`.\n\nMeasured over the 505 flagged tickets by enumerating those per-ticket deletion records:\n504 have records covering every cited-missing uuid, 1 partial, 0 with no record at all.\n\nValidated end-to-end on a COPY of the live store: restoring every event file the per-ticket\ndeletion scan reports, then rebuilding, restored 214/214 of the already-regressed tickets to\ntheir correct status (486 events recovered).\n\n## Why the snapshot's citation list is not enough\nTicket 72d5 recovered its close STATUS but still read in_progress: an EARLIER deleted STATUS\nthat no surviving snapshot cites broke the chain's current_status precondition, so the\nreducer rejected both it and the close. Recovery must sweep the ticket's full deletion\nhistory, NOT the newest snapshot's source_event_uuids.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] `recover_deleted_sources(tracker, ticket_id, ticket_dir)` restores every event file\n      deleted from the ticket dir in tickets-branch history, enumerated by a per-ticket\n      `git log --diff-filter=D -- <tid>/` scan and read at the deleting commit's parent,\n      writing each back as a folded `.retired` source (never resurrecting it as a live event).\n- [ ] The fsck repair path attempts recovery BEFORE deciding to refuse: recover -> re-check\n      `snapshot_missing_sources` -> rebuild if now complete; only refuse (b636 fail-closed)\n      when recovery cannot complete the log.\n- [ ] `--dry-run` reports what WOULD be recovered and rebuilt, writing nothing.\n- [ ] Recovery is scoped per ticket (no full-history index build) so it stays usable on a\n      large store.\n- [ ] Held-out test: a ticket whose close exists ONLY in history is recovered and reads\n      closed again after `fsck --repair`.\n- [ ] A ticket with genuinely unrecoverable sources still refuses and is surfaced."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786260182599456001,"uuid":"614bcb4c-fdae-43ba-9aac-e33eaf0aed08"}